	'st_n' => '06',
	'st_note' => 'These are three people’s experiences, not promises.',
	'st_sub' => 'What changing trade looked like for three of them.',
	'stn1_n' => 'Aiyana Brooks',
	'stn1_p' => 'Aiyana fixed the café’s water heater with a plumber on the phone and decided to learn how. In Year 2 she joined a four-person heating firm in Wrenfield, and she now works there, mostly on boiler services.',
	'stn1_r' => 'was a café manager · placed with a heating firm',
	'stn2_n' => 'Farid Haddad',
	'stn2_p' => 'Farid was slow at soldering for the first term and spent several Fridays at the bench with Hana. He was placed with a housing maintenance team, and the team kept him on after the placement year.',
	'stn2_r' => 'worked in a warehouse · placed with a housing team',
	'stn3_n' => 'Ingrid Solberg',
	'stn3_p' => 'Ingrid was the oldest in her group. She was placed with a heat-pump installer and now plans the pipe runs on site before the crew arrives, with a bench habit of checking every joint twice.',
	'stn3_r' => 'returned to work after raising three children',
	'stories_h' => 'Apprentice stories',
	'stories_lede' => 'Three apprentices describe the two years from inside the course: Aiyana Brooks, Farid Haddad, and Ingrid Solberg. These are three people\'s experiences, not promises.',
	'stories_note_p' => 'Aiyana Brooks, Farid Haddad, and Ingrid Solberg each finished the two years, and each stayed in work they had met on placement. The college records that because it happened to them. The same page states the rule in full. A placement is a training year, not a job offer. Many firms do keep their apprentice on; we never promise it. No employment rate is published. No award is attached to their names. What the college signs for every apprentice, including these three, is the bench assessments and the signed logbook, EUR 220 per year. The structure they went through is still the structure on offer: a September intake, Year 1 at the bench for EUR 4,800, Year 2 with one of 18 firms for EUR 2,400.',
	'stories_note_t' => 'These are three people\'s experiences, not promises.',
	'stories_rail' => 'STORIES · THREE',
	'stories_s1_h' => 'Aiyana Brooks',
	'stories_s1_p1' => 'Aiyana Brooks was 34 and a café manager when she decided to change trade. The turn came after she fixed the café\'s water heater with a plumber on the phone. She started at Kikan College with no previous trade experience, which the course does not ask for, and she was well over the minimum age of 18. Year 1 was the bench year in Wrenfield: 38 weeks from September to July, four days in the halls and Friday on theory.',
	'stories_s1_p2' => 'Year 2 placed her with a four-person heating firm in Wrenfield, four days a week with the firm and Fridays at college. She now works there, mostly on boiler services. She says, "I like that a day ends with something that works." That is her path. A placement is a training year, not a job offer. Many firms do keep their apprentice on; we never promise it.',
	'stories_s2_h' => 'Farid Haddad',
	'stories_s2_p1' => 'Farid Haddad was 27 and working in a warehouse. He had no trade behind him. Soldering was slow for the whole of his first term. Hana Petrović kept him back on Fridays, on the open bench that runs from 13:15 to 15:30, until the joints were sound enough to leave in the logbook. He was placed with a housing maintenance team, one of the five teams among the 18 partners within 40 km of Wrenfield.',
	'stories_s2_p2' => 'The team kept him on after the placement year. Read that as what happened to him, not as a rate the next group can claim. Many firms do keep their apprentice on; we never promise it. His Year 1 still looked like the timetable everyone else had: Monday to Thursday, 07:45–15:30, 38 teaching weeks, in an intake of 24. The college does not treat his catching-up as a method it will repeat for someone else.',
	'stories_s3_h' => 'Ingrid Solberg',
	'stories_s3_p1' => 'Ingrid Solberg was 46 when she returned to work after raising three children. She was the oldest in her group. There is no upper age on the course, and the minimum is 18, so a group of 12 can hold that spread. The intake is 24 places, two groups. She was placed with a heat-pump installer, one of the two firms of that kind among the 18 partners within 40 km of Wrenfield. She now plans the pipe runs on site.',
	'stories_s3_p2' => 'Year 2 followed the college pattern: four days a week with the firm, Friday at college, and a site visit from Grace Mwangi each term. Where gas or electrical work arose, it was only under the firm\'s qualified engineer. The college can say what she does now. It does not say the next person placed with a heat-pump installer will plan runs, or will be kept on.',
	'stories_s5_h' => 'Next steps',
	'stories_s5_p1' => 'Hearing a story is not the same as a day in the hall. Taster Saturday, the first Saturday of every month except August, runs 09:00–15:00, lunch included, and it is free. Boots and glasses are provided, and no booking is needed for the morning. A Tuesday visit at 16:00 is the shorter look. Applications for September close on 30 April, by the form or by email to hello@kikan-college.example.',
	'stories_s5_p2' => 'The bench morning lasts 90 minutes. You cut and join one piece of pipe, and nobody expects it to be good. An offer letter comes within 10 working days, and a EUR 300 deposit holds the place and counts towards Year 1. The office is at 22 Foundry Lane, Wrenfield, Monday to Friday, 08:00–16:30.',
